problems.problem:
The numbers $a_1,a_2,a_3$ are distinct positive integers such that

(i) $a_1$ divides $a_2+a_3+a_2a_3$;

(ii) $a_2$ divides $a_3+a_1+a_3a_1$;

(iii) $a_3$ divides $a_1+a_2+a_1a_2$.

Prove that $a_1,a_2,a_3$ cannot all be prime.

Consider the function $f$ defined by $$f(n)=\frac{1}{n}\left(\left\lfloor\frac{n}{1}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor+\cdots+\left\lfloor\frac{n}{n}\right\rfloor \right)$$ for all positive integers $n$. Prove that

(a) $f(n+1)>f(n)$ for infinitely many $n$;

(b) $f(n+1)<f(n)$ for infinitely many $n$.

Let $ABC$ be a triangle and $PQRS$ a square with $P$ on $AB$, $Q$ on $AC$, and $R$ and $S$ on $BC$. Let $H$ be the point on $BC$ such that $AH$ is the altitude from $A$. Prove that

(a) $\frac{1}{AH} +\frac{1}{BC}=\frac{1}{PQ}$;

(b) the area of $ABC$ is twice the area of $PQRS$ if and only if $AH=BC$.

Let $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{R}$ be a function satisfying

(i) $f(n) \ge 0$ for all $n \in \mathbb{Z}$;

(ii) $f(mn)=f(m)f(n)$ for all $m,n \in \mathbb{Z}$;

(iii) $f(m+n) \le \max(f(m),f(n))$ for all $m,n \in \mathbb{Z}$.

(a) Prove that $f(n) \le 1$ for all $n \in \mathbb{Z}$.

(b) Find a function $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying (i), (ii), (iii), $0<f(2)<1$ and $f(2007) = 1$.

A game is played on a board with an infinite row of holes labelled $0, 1, 2, \ldots$. Initially $2009$ pebbles are put into hole $1$; the other holes are empty. Steps are performed according to the following rules:

(i) at each step, two pebbles are removed from one of the holes (if possible), and one pebble is put into each of the neighbouring holes;

(ii) no pebbles are ever removed from hole $0$;

(iii) the game ends when no hole with a positive label contains at least two pebbles.

Show that the game always terminates and that the number of pebbles in hole $0$ at the end of the game does not depend on the specific sequence of steps. Determine this number.

Let $A$ denote the set of real numbers $x$ with $0\le x<1$. A function $f:A\to \mathbb{R}$ has the properties

(i) $f(x)=2f\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)$ for all $x\in A$;

(ii) $f(x)=1-f\left(x-\frac{1}{2}\right)$ if $\frac{1}{2}\le x<1$.

Prove that

(a) $f(x)+f(1-x)\ge \frac{2}{3}$ if $x$ is rational and $0<x<1$;

(b) there are infinitely many odd positive integers $q$ such that equality holds in (a) for $x=\frac{1}{q}$.

Let $\mathbb{N}_0$ denote the set of all nonnegative integers. Determine all functions $f:\mathbb{N}_0\to\mathbb{N}_0$ with the following two properties:

• $0\le f(x)\le x^2$ for all $x\in\mathbb{N}_0$;

• $x-y$ divides $f(x)-f(y)$ for all $x,y\in\mathbb{N}_0$ with $x>y$.

At the start of the first game of the season of the Mighty Mathematicians Football Team, the coach noticed that the jersey numbers of the $22$ players on the field were all the numbers from $1$ to $22$. At halftime, the coach substituted her goalkeeper, with jersey number $1$, by a reserve player. No other substitutions were made by either team at or before halftime. The coach noticed that after the substitution no two players on the field had the same jersey number and that the sums of the jersey numbers of the two teams were exactly equal. Determine

• the greatest possible jersey number of the reserve player;

• the smallest possible (positive) jersey number of the reserve player.

Let $n \geq 3$ be an integer, and consider a set of $n$ points in three-dimensional space such that

(i) every two distinct points are connected by a string which is red, green, blue or yellow;

(ii) for every three distinct points, if the three strings between them are not all of the same colour, then they are of three different colours;

(iii) not all the strings have the same colour.

Find the maximum possible value of $n$.

A sequence of letters, each of which is $A$, $B$, $C$ or $D$, is called alternating if

• between every two $A$'s there is at least one $B$;

• between every two $B$'s there is at least one $A$;

• between every two $C$'s there is at least one $D$;

• between every two $D$'s there is at least one $C$.

Calculate the number of alternating sequences of length $6$.

Let $n \geqslant 2$ be an integer, and let $r_n$ be the remainder when $n^{n - 1} - 1$ is divided by $(n - 1)^3$. Prove that

(a) for each $n \geqslant 2$, $r_n$ is divisible by $m^2$ for some integer $m \geqslant 2$;

(b) there exist infinitely many $n \geqslant 2$ for which $r_n=m^2$ for some integer $m \geqslant 2$.
